,0,1,7,4,0,5,0,0,7],[3,3,3,3,7,7,0,0,0,7,4,0,8,0,0,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,0,0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[9,0,7,7,7,7,0,0,0,7,9,0,0,0,0,0],[0,8,7,0,0,7,0,5,0,7,0,0,0,6,5,0],[0,0,7,0,0,7,2,0,4,7,0,2,0,0,0,0],[0,0,7,0,0,7,0,0,0,7,0,0,4,0,8,0],[0,0,7,0,5,7,0,1,0,7,0,9,4,0,5,0],[6,0,7,0,5,7,0,0,9,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,5,0,7,0,0,1,7,7,0,0,0,0,0],[5,0,7,5,0,7,0,9,0,7,7,0,0,9,0,1],[0,0,7,0,5,7,0,9,8,7,7,1,0,4,0,0],[5,0,7,0,4,7,0,0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,0,2,7,0,0,5,7,7,7,7,7,7,7]],"output":[[7,2,7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[7,7,5,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,0,0,0,0,0,7],[7,4,7,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,0,5,0,0,7],[3,3,3,3,7,7,7,7,7,7,4,0,8,0,0,7],[7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[9,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,8,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[6,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,0,0,0,0,0],[5,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,0,0,9,0,1],[0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,1,0,4,0,0],[5,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7],[0,0,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7,7]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"**What the grid is made of.** Every grid has a small legend in the top-left corner. It is enclosed by a frame of colour 3: row 3 and column 3. The cells inside that frame are mostly background (7), plus a few coloured dots. The rest of the canvas holds several \"rectangles\": blocks of 0 sprinkled with coloured dots, separated from each other by background 7. In pair 3 and in the test, 0 is the most common colour, so the background has to be taken as the colour outside the rectangles (7), not as the most frequent colour.\n\n**What changes.** Some rectangles are erased entirely (painted 7), and the others are left untouched. To find out which, I compared the set of dot colours in each rectangle with the colours in the legend:\n\n- **Pair 1.** The legend colours are {4, 1, 2}.\n  - Erased: the rectangle at rows 1–5, cols 12–14 has {4, 1, 2}. The one at rows 6–9 has {9, 5, 2, 1, 4, 8}. The one at rows 11–14, cols 0–7 has {1, 9, 5, 8, 6, 4, 2}. Each of these contains all three legend colours.\n  - Kept: the rectangle at top, cols 7–9 has {9, 6, 4, 5}, with no 1 or 2. The one at bottom, cols 9–12 has {9, 8, 6, 5, 2}, with no 4 or 1. The one at right, cols 14–15 has {1, 4, 6, 8}, with no 2.\n- **Pair 2.** The legend colours are {9, 5, 6}. Only the bottom-right rectangle {9, 6, 5, 1, 8} contains all three, and it is the only one erased.\n- **Pair 3.** The legend colours are {6, 1, 8}. Only the large top-right rectangle contains 6, 1 and 8, and it is erased. The rectangle at rows 5–10 has {5, 2, 1, 9, 6} but lacks 8, so it is kept.\n\n**Rule.** Erase (paint with 7) every rectangle whose colours include all of the legend's colours. Leave everything else unchanged.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Locate the frame from the cells of colour 3. Read the legend colours as the cells inside the frame's bounding box that are neither 3 nor 7.\n2. Find the 4-connected components of non-7 cells, skipping any component that touches the legend box.\n3. Erase each component whose colour set contains the whole legend set. I emit one stage per erased rectangle.\n\nThe components rely on the rectangles being separated by 7. That holds in all train pairs. In the test they are separated by the 7 columns at x = 2, 5 and 9 and the 7 row at y = 10 on the right side.\n\n**Test.** The legend colours are {2, 5, 4}. Any rectangle containing 2, 5 and 4 together gets erased; all others stay as they are.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = 7\n    threes = [(y, x) for y, x in zip(*np.where(g == 3))]\n    y0, x0, y1, x1 = bbox(threes)\n    legend = set(int(v) for v in g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1].ravel()) - {3, bg}\n    out = g.copy()\n    for comp in components(g != bg):\n        if any(y0 <= y <= y1 and x0 <= x <= x1 for y, x in comp):\n            continue\n        cols = set(int(g[y, x]) for y, x in comp)\n        if legend <= cols:\n            for y, x in comp:\n                out[y, x] = bg\n            emit(out, f\"Erase the rectangle at {bbox(comp)}: it contains every legend colour {sorted(legend)}.\")\n    emit(out, \"Final grid: rectangles containing all legend colours are removed.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Find the 4-connected components of non-7 cells, skipping any component that touches the legend box."}],"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In pair 3 and in the test, 0 is the most common colour, so the background has to be taken as the colour outside the rectangles (7), not as the most frequent colour."}],"legend":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the grid is made of.** Every grid has a small legend in the top-left corner."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"To find out which, I compared the set of dot colours in each rectangle with the colours in the legend:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"- **Pair 1.** The legend colours are {4, 1, 2}."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is enclosed by a frame of colour 3: row 3 and column 3."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Read the legend colours as the cells inside the frame's bounding box that are neither 3 nor 7."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"144b76fc04b21d5212c8bc951282b713037bb4a66c36b93029cf2d33401d7ae2","task_sha256":"0e35f911f036147de16a1f66a6c9720b44a17c2e0a6674a621725bf05b1068f8","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
